  f_renal_function notes ```
The GFR relative to the normal GFR of the individual; 1.0 normal, < 1.0 reduced.
An initial value, as the covariates. Model default without a source.
```
  f_cardiac_function notes ```
The cardiac output relative to the normal cardiac output of the individual; it multiplies
`CO` and so every blood flow; 1.0 normal, < 1.0 reduced. An initial value, as the
covariates. Model default without a source.
```
  f_shunt notes ```
The fraction in [0, 1] of the hepatic arterial and portal venous inflow which bypasses
the liver into the venous blood; 0.0 healthy, the hepatic impairment sets it. An
initial value, as the covariates. Model default without a source.
```
  fcp_shunt notes ```
Portosystemic shunt of liver cirrhosis (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B, C,
linear in between), the values of the classes: A 0.3994897959183674, B 0.6979591836734694,
C 0.8127551020408164; portosystemic shunt `f_shunt` of the classes A, B, C (healthy 0):
the values of pkpdsim.impairment.CIRRHOSIS (bit-identical here; the MathML of the
SBML writes 15 significant digits), the degrees of cirrhosis of the Child-Pugh classes
of the hand-written ICG models; their publication is not verified; replaces the reduced
portal vein flow of Edginton & Willmann 2008 (Table I: 0.40, 0.36, 0.04 of the
controls, from the portal perfusion of Annet 2003), see the header

`fcp_shunt = piecewise(0.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 0.0 dimensionless + 0.3994897959183674 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.3994897959183674 dimensionless + 0.298469387755102 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.6979591836734694 dimensionless + 0.11479591836734693 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.8127551020408164 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fcp_functional_liver notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the functional liver (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes
A, B, C, linear in between), the values of the classes: A 0.8982, B 0.7854, C 0.6655;
the liver tissue of the classes A, B, C relative to healthy, `f_functional`, which scales the
tissue volume of the liver and the capacity of its processes: the liver volume, so
that the loss of hepatocytes is counted once; the factors of the enzymes and transporters
are abundances per amount of liver tissue, of cirrhotic tissue with fewer hepatocytes
per gram, and the capacity of the liver is that abundance times the amount of tissue.
The functional liver mass of Edginton & Willmann 2008 (Table I: 0.69, 0.55, 0.28,
a hepatocyte radioligand, Virgolini et al.) counts the hepatocytes and would count
their loss a second time with the abundances per tissue; Johnson 2010 (Table III)
take the functional hepatocyte volume (0.81, 0.65, 0.53) with abundances per mg of
microsomal protein of the functional liver; the hepatocyte volume of Kwon 2001 is
0.86 and 0.82 (A, B); the consensus of the studies of the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`):
the ratio of the liver volume per body weight (liver_volume_per_bw) of the class
to the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis;
A 0.898 (95 % CI 0.821-0.982; 2 studies: Kwon2001 0.921, Lin1998 0.827), from a physiological
or clinical quantity of the subjects; B 0.785 (95 % CI 0.558-1.11; 2 studies: Kwon2001
0.936, Lin1998 0.661), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
C 0.666 (95 % CI 0.545-0.813; 1 study: Lin1998 0.666), from a physiological or clinical
quantity of the subjects

`fcp_functional_liver = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.1018 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.8982 dimensionless + -0.11280000000000001 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.7854 dimensionless + -0.1199 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.6655 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Kwon HA, Matsui Y, Ha-Kawa SK, Kamiyama Y. Functional Hepatic Volume Measured by Technetium-99M-Galactosyl-Human Serum Albumin Liver Scintigraphy: Comparison Between Hepatocyte Volume and Liver Volume by Computed Tomography. Am J Gastroenterol 96(2):541-546, 2001. doi:10.1111/j.1572-0241.2001.03556.x
- Lin XZ, Sun YN, Liu YH, Sheu BS, Cheng BN, Chen CY, Tsai HM, Shen CL. Liver volume in patients with or without chronic liver diseases. Hepatogastroenterology 45(22):1069-1074, 1998. PMID 9756008.
```
  BW_ref notes ```
Body weight of the reference individual of the age. Of the reference individual of
35 years (the build has no physiology over age, `PBPKDefinition.age`): children (newborn, 1, 5, 10,
15 years): Table 2.9, mass, female; the adult from 20 years: Table 2.9, adult female;
linear between the ages

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  HEIGHT_ref notes ```
Height of the reference individual of the age. Of the reference individual of 35
years (the build has no physiology over age, `PBPKDefinition.age`): children (newborn, 1, 5, 10, 15
years): Table 2.9, height, female; the adult from 20 years: Table 2.9, adult female;
linear between the ages

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  BSA_ref notes ```
Body surface area of the reference individual of the age, Du Bois formula; the reference
of the skin.

`BSA_ref = 0.007184 m2 * (BW_ref / 1 kg)^0.425 * (HEIGHT_ref / 1 cm)^0.725` 

Sources:

- Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. Clinical calorimetry, tenth paper: a formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 17(6_2):863-871, 1916. doi:10.1001/archinte.1916.00080130010002
```
  fage_FFM notes ```
1 at 35 years, the age of the individual of a build without the physiology over age
(`PBPKDefinition.age`, which gives the curve of `pkpdsim.pbpk.age`): the multiplier of the Janmahasatian 2005 fat-free
mass of girls, 1.11 + (1 - 1.11)/(1 + (AGE/7.1)^-1.1); model M2 of Al-Sallami 2015
Table 4, read from the paper: a = 1.11 (bootstrap 95 % CI 1.09-1.16), c = 1.1 (1.1,
0.7-1.6) and A50 = 7.1 years (7.0, 3.5-10.3), with b fixed to 1. The index dataset
is 926 dual energy X-ray absorptiometry measurements of 496 females of a median age
of 11.6 years (range 3-29.3) and a median body mass index of 19.1 kg/m2 (12.6-41.3),
the external test dataset 90 females; the factor is above 1 because the adult equation
under-predicts the fat-free mass of girls. Relative to 20 years, 1 from 20 years;
extrapolated below 3 years. Against the reference children of Fomon 1982 Table I
the chain gives 0.94 to 0.91 of their fat-free mass over 3 to 10 years, so it puts
the body fat of a girl at 23 to 27 % against their 16 to 19 %.

Sources:

- Al-Sallami HS, Goulding A, Grant A, Taylor R, Holford N, Duffull SB. Prediction of fat-free mass in children. Clin Pharmacokinet 54(11):1169-1178, 2015. doi:10.1007/s40262-015-0277-z
- Fomon SJ, Haschke F, Ziegler EE, Nelson SE. Body composition of reference children from birth to age 10 years. Am J Clin Nutr 35(5):1169-1175, 1982. doi:10.1093/ajcn/35.5.1169
```
  FFM_ref notes ```
Fat-free mass of the reference individual of the age, the female formula of Janmahasatian
2005 times the factor of the children (Al-Sallami 2015).

`FFM_ref = BW_ref * 9270 dimensionless / (8780 dimensionless + 244 dimensionless * (BW_ref / 1 kg) / (HEIGHT_ref / 100 cm)^2) * fage_FFM` 

Sources:

- Janmahasatian S, Duffull SB, Ash S, Ward LC, Byrne NM, Green B. Quantification of lean bodyweight. Clin Pharmacokinet 44(10):1051-1065, 2005. doi:10.2165/00003088-200544100-00004
- Al-Sallami HS, Goulding A, Grant A, Taylor R, Holford N, Duffull SB. Prediction of fat-free mass in children. Clin Pharmacokinet 54(11):1169-1178, 2015. doi:10.1007/s40262-015-0277-z
```
  CO_ref notes ```
Cardiac output of the reference individual of the age. Of the reference individual
of 35 years (the build has no physiology over age, `PBPKDefinition.age`): children (newborn, 1, 5,
10, 15 years): Table 2.39 (Section 7.5), cardiac output, female; the adult from 20
years: Table 2.39, adult; linear between the ages; times the aging of the adults:
cardiac index at a fixed body surface area: 1 - 0.0030 \* (age - 35) (cardiovascular
magnetic resonance of Carlsson 2012, -0.21 to -0.29 % per year; the three-dimensional
echocardiography of Patel 2021 about -0.25 % per year); both sexes alike; the decline
is debated, 1.0 to 0.64 at 80 years in the literature

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
- Carlsson M, Andersson R, Bloch KM, Steding-Ehrenborg K, Mosén H, Stahlberg F, Ekmehag B, Arheden H. Cardiac output and cardiac index measured with cardiovascular magnetic resonance in healthy subjects, elite athletes and patients with congestive heart failure. J Cardiovasc Magn Reson 14(1):51, 2012. doi:10.1186/1532-429X-14-51
```
  GFR_ref notes ```
GFR of the adult reference individual.

- female: derived: ICRP 89 Section 8.3.1 para 380 gives 125 ml/min for the adult male and a GFR roughly proportional to body size and lower in females; scaled with the body surface area of Table 2.9, 125 \* 1.66/1.90


Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  Vblood_ref notes ```
Total blood volume of the reference individual of the age. Of the reference individual
of 35 years (the build has no physiology over age, `PBPKDefinition.age`): children (newborn, 1, 5,
10, 15 years): Section 7.4, total blood volume, females; the adult from 20 years:
Section 7.4, total blood volume, adult; linear between the ages

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  fage_HCT notes ```
1 at 35 years, the age of the individual of a build without the physiology over age
(`PBPKDefinition.age`, which gives the curve of `pkpdsim.pbpk.age`): the hematocrit (fraction) of a newborn at term:
days 0, 7, 14 and 28 of Scholkmann 2019, Equation 2 evaluated as printed at a gestational
age of 40 weeks (the fit of about 100 000 neonates of Henry and Christensen 2015);
the nadir 0.35 at 2 months of the PK-Sim database (secondary, the source of the value
not read); from 1 year the table of NHANES. Jopling 2009 (Intermountain Healthcare,
41 957 hematocrit and 39 559 hemoglobin values of the first 28 days, every patient
who received an erythrocyte transfusion excluded) confirms two things read before
from its abstract only: there is no effect of sex on the hematocrit or the hemoglobin
of the neonate, which the authors state they can now conclude confidently from this
sample size, and the fall over the first 28 days, their term group (35-42 weeks)
going from a mean hemoglobin of 18 g/dl (5th percentile 14) at birth to 13 g/dl (5th
percentile 9.5) at 28 days, a ratio of 0.72 against the 0.385/0.522 = 0.74 of this
curve. Their regression of the hematocrit on the gestational age within 6 hours of
birth, 28.59 + 0.6359\*GA in weeks (25 464 values), gives 54.0 % at 40 weeks against
the 52.2 % here, and their reference range of the hematocrit at birth in term neonates
is 42 to 65 %. Not modelled: the hematocrit of a late preterm or term neonate rises
by 3.6 +- 0.5 % during the first four hours after delivery (placental transfusion),
so day 0 is the value within hours of birth; the ratio of the hematocrit of the sex
to its value at 35 years (blood_nhanes.toml, 1 to 80 years); the respondents of 80
years and over are placed at 85 years, a model choice (their mean age is not published);
constant beyond

Sources:

- Scholkmann F, Ostojic D, Isler H, Bassler D, Wolf M, Karen T. Reference ranges for hemoglobin and hematocrit levels in neonates as a function of gestational age (22-42 weeks) and postnatal age (0-29 days): mathematical modeling. Children 6(3):38, 2019. doi:10.3390/children6030038
- Jopling J, Henry E, Wiedmeier SE, Christensen RD. Reference ranges for hematocrit and blood hemoglobin concentration during the neonatal period: data from a multihospital health care system. Pediatrics 123(2):e333-e337, 2009. doi:10.1542/peds.2008-2654
- Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Health Statistics. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) 2013-2014, 2015-2016 and 2017-2018, demographics (DEMO), body measures (BMX), complete blood count (CBC) and standard biochemistry profile (BIOPRO) of the cycles H, I and J (public domain). https://wwwn.cdc.gov/nchs/nhanes/ (accessed 2026-11-06). Read from the public files by scripts/database/nhanes_populations.py, scripts/database/nhanes_blood.py and scripts/database/nhanes_waist.py.
```
  HCT_ref notes ```
The volume fraction of the blood cells in blood of the healthy individual of the
age: the adult value times `fage_HCT`.

- female: Section 7.3 para 309, red blood cells about 38 % (37-48 %) of the blood volume of adult females; Table 2.12: 1500 ml of 3900 ml


`HCT_ref = 0.38 dimensionless * fage_HCT` 

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  fimp_HCT notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the hematocrit of
the sex of the build; the anaemia of cirrhosis and of chronic kidney disease combine
multiplicatively.

`fimp_HCT = fcp_hematocrit * fckd_hematocrit`
```
