  fage_Qbr notes ```
1 at 35 years, the age of the individual of a build without the physiology over age
(`PBPKDefinition.age`, which gives the curve of `pkpdsim.pbpk.age`): the global cerebral blood flow per mass relative
to the adults (51 ml/min/100 g of 32 adults of 18-29 years), Xe-133 SPECT of 42 neurologically
normal children (Table 1: 2-45 days 50, 2-7 months 55, 9-15 months 60, 16-22 months
62, 2-4 years 65, 6-19 years 62 ml/min/100 g, at the middle of each group; the maximum
71 at 5 years and the adult value after 19 years of the text); the brain takes 47
% of the cardiac output at 1 year (Wu 2016: 45.5 % below 4 years by 4D flow MRI,
14.5 % in adults); the fraction of the cardiac output to the liver (hepatic artery
and portal organs) follows the liver volume (liver_mass), so the hepatic blood flow
is the cardiac output times the liver volume factor, 0.72 at 80 years; a model choice
of the form after Imamura 2026. Zoli 1999 Table 1 (40 healthy subjects within 10
% of the ideal body weight, 10 per group, echo-Doppler of the portal vein and the
common hepatic artery, functional flow by the hepatic clearance of D-sorbitol) measures,
for the age groups below 45, 45-60, 61-75 and above 75 years: portal flow 1209 +-
205, 980 +- 337, 978 +- 194 and 749 +- 117 ml/min (ANOVA p = 0.0018), hepatic artery
flow 235 +- 76, 313 +- 117, 318 +- 144 and 271 +- 94 ml/min (p = 0.3632, not significant),
total hepatic flow 1445 +- 220, 1293 +- 340, 1297 +- 253 and 1020 +- 148 ml/min (p
= 0.0088) and functional hepatic flow 1514 +- 250, 1326 +- 348, 1258 +- 226 and 1015
+- 163 ml/min (p = 0.0023). The total flow above 75 years is 1020/1445 = 0.706 of
the flow below 45 years and the functional flow 1015/1514 = 0.670; the two are correlated
over the whole population (r = 0.871), so the fall is a fall of the flow and not
an intrahepatic shunt. The decline is in the portal limb: the hepatic artery flow
does not change significantly (a mild rise between 45 and 75 years), while the library
applies one factor to the hepatic artery and the portal organs together, which is
a known simplification. The library&apos;s factor is also shallower than these data
at the oldest ages (0.835 at 80 years against the 0.706 of the group above 75 years),
because its form follows the liver volume of Imamura 2026 and is anchored at 50 years;
renal plasma flow: 1 to 40 years, -0.012 per year to 85 years (fitted to the effective
renal plasma flow of Davies and Shock 1950, men; Fliser 1993 0.52 at 70 against 26
years); beyond 85 years the filtration fraction is held, the flow follows the GFR
of men (0.46 \* 0.578/0.6796 at 100 years), a model choice: the line would give a
filtration fraction of 0.36; no reliable sex difference (Berg 2006, abstract), both
sexes alike, held at the filtration fraction of men beyond 85 years; cardiac index
at a fixed body surface area: 1 - 0.0030 \* (age - 35) (cardiovascular magnetic resonance
of Carlsson 2012, -0.21 to -0.29 % per year; the three-dimensional echocardiography
of Patel 2021 about -0.25 % per year); both sexes alike; the decline is debated,
1.0 to 0.64 at 80 years in the literature

Sources:

- Chiron C, Raynaud C, Maziere B, Zilbovicius M, Laflamme L, Masure MC, Dulac O, Bourguignon M, Syrota A. Changes in regional cerebral blood flow during brain maturation in children and adolescents. J Nucl Med 33(5):696-703, 1992. PMID 1569478.
- Zoli M, Magalotti D, Bianchi G, Gueli C, Orlandini C, Grimaldi M, Marchesini G. Total and functional hepatic blood flow decrease in parallel with ageing. Age Ageing 28(1):29-33, 1999. doi:10.1093/ageing/28.1.29
- Davies DF, Shock NW. Age changes in glomerular filtration rate, effective renal plasma flow, and tubular excretory capacity in adult males. J Clin Invest 29(5):496-507, 1950. doi:10.1172/JCI102286
- Carlsson M, Andersson R, Bloch KM, Steding-Ehrenborg K, Mosén H, Stahlberg F, Ekmehag B, Arheden H. Cardiac output and cardiac index measured with cardiovascular magnetic resonance in healthy subjects, elite athletes and patients with congestive heart failure. J Cardiovasc Magn Reson 14(1):51, 2012. doi:10.1186/1532-429X-14-51
```
  fage_Qli notes ```
