  fVpo notes ```
Volume of the blood pool as fraction of the blood volume.

- female: derived: the adult portal vein as cylinder of mean length 48.63 mm and diameter 10.72 mm (Chau 2023, abstract) holds 4.39 ml, divided by the blood volume; taken from the large veins of ICRP 89 Table 2.14, which has no portal vein of its own


Sources:

- Chau P, Yoon JS, Moses D, Pather N. A systematic review and meta-analysis of portal vein morphometry in pediatric and adult populations: drawing the line between normal and abnormal findings. Eur J Radiol 168:111016, 2023. doi:10.1016/j.ejrad.2023.111016
```
  fsex_CO notes ```
The factor of the sex on the cardiac output of the female individual of 35 years
relative to a man of the same body weight and height (`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences`): The cardiac index (cardiac
output per body surface area) of women and men of the same size is the same: Williams
1994 (the source of the cardiac output of ICRP 89; Table 5, the same reference cardiac
index of both sexes at every age, 3.7 l/min/m2 at 24 and 3.6 at 34 years, the 6.5
and 5.9 l/min of the adults the different body sizes), Carlsson 2012 (CMR, n = 144,
3.1 +- 0.4 women and 3.2 +- 0.5 l/min/m2 men, p = 0.64), Patel 2021 (WASE, n = 605,
18-40 years, equal), de Simone 1997 (no sex effect at the same body surface area
or weight, children and adults) and Middlemiss 2019 (inert gas rebreathing, n = 600,
20-49 years, women 0.94-0.96 at the same size). The scaling of the cardiac output
with the lean tissue of the sex (Huisinga 2012) gives a woman about 9 % more cardiac
output than a man of the same body weight and height (women have more cardiac output
per fat-free mass, 20-25 % in Middlemiss 2019 and Yeon 2015, and less fat-free mass
at the same size); the factor removes it, computed from the physiology at every age
of the form `same_cardiac_index` (`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences.same_cardiac_index`): the male over the female cardiac output of the scaling at the
body weight and height between the reference individuals of the age, linear between
the ages; 0.914 of the adults (a direct fit of the library to the 12 groups of Middlemiss
2019 gives 0.911, the range of the evidence 0.88-0.96), near 1 in the children, whose
references of ICRP 89 have the same cardiac output in both sexes until 10 years (Williams
1994 p. 14: independent of sex until near 10 years), and removing the drift of the
references over puberty; the same in the elderly (no consistent sex difference of
the decline: Carlsson 2012, Middlemiss 2019, Williams 1994).

Sources:

- Williams LR. Reference values for total blood volume and cardiac output in humans. Report ORNL/TM-12814, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Oak Ridge, TN, 1994. OSTI ID 10186900, https://www.osti.gov/biblio/10186900 (US government report, public).
- Carlsson M, Andersson R, Bloch KM, Steding-Ehrenborg K, Mosén H, Stahlberg F, Ekmehag B, Arheden H. Cardiac output and cardiac index measured with cardiovascular magnetic resonance in healthy subjects, elite athletes and patients with congestive heart failure. J Cardiovasc Magn Reson 14(1):51, 2012. doi:10.1186/1532-429X-14-51
- Patel HN, Miyoshi T, Addetia K, Henry MP, Citro R, Daimon M, Gutierrez Fajardo P, Kasliwal RR, Kirkpatrick JN, Monaghan MJ, et al. Normal values of cardiac output and stroke volume according to measurement technique, age, sex, and ethnicity: results of the World Alliance of Societies of Echocardiography study. J Am Soc Echocardiogr 34(10):1077-1085.e1, 2021. doi:10.1016/j.echo.2021.05.012
- Middlemiss JE, Cocks A, Paapstel K, Maki-Petaja KM, Sunita, Wilkinson IB, McEniery CM; ACCT Study Investigators. Evaluation of inert gas rebreathing for determination of cardiac output: influence of age, gender and body size. Hypertens Res 42(6):834-844, 2019. doi:10.1038/s41440-018-0179-1
- de Simone G, Devereux RB, Daniels SR, Mureddu G, Roman MJ, Kimball TR, Greco R, Witt S, Contaldo F. Stroke volume and cardiac output in normotensive children and adults: assessment of relations with body size and impact of overweight. Circulation 95(7):1837-1843, 1997. doi:10.1161/01.CIR.95.7.1837
```
  fsex_t_stomach_fed notes ```
The factor of the sex on the transit time of the stomach in the fed state of the
female individual of 35 years relative to a man of the same body weight and height
(`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences`): The emptying of a meal (caloric liquids and solids) of premenopausal women
is slower than of men: scintigraphy of solids, Camilleri 2012 (n = 319, 127.7 +-
28.7 women and 109.9 +- 28.6 min men, Table 2A, 1.16 of the means and 1.19 of the
medians, sex significant after age and BMI) and Vasavid 2014 (n = 189, 1.17); the
smaller studies of premenopausal women 1.42-1.78 (Datz 1987, Hutson 1989), rounded
to 1.33 (caloric liquids) and 1.40 (solids) by ICRP 89 (para 275); 1.2 of the large
cohorts on the fed transit time of the stomach (`T_st_fed`). The fasted emptying of water
does not differ (ICRP 89, 30 min both sexes; no sex difference of the fasting motility).
Children before puberty 1 (ICRP 89 children with the values of the men, Hauser 2016
no sex effect at 1-17 years), the difference from puberty (Karrento 2025, n = 216,
half time 86.6 girls and 73.6 min boys, 1.18), linear from 10 to 15 years; postmenopausal
women without hormone replacement empty solids like men (Hutson 1989, Gryback 2000,
Vasavid 2014), linear from 45 to 55 years, a choice.

Sources:

- Camilleri M, Iturrino J, Bharucha AE, Burton D, Shin A, Jeong ID, Zinsmeister AR. Performance characteristics of scintigraphic measurement of gastric emptying of solids in healthy participants. Neurogastroenterol Motil 24(12):1076-e562, 2012. doi:10.1111/j.1365-2982.2012.01972.x
- Vasavid P, Chaiwatanarat T, Pusuwan P, Sritara C, Roysri K, Namwongprom S, Kuanrakcharoen P, Premprabha T, Chunlertrith K, Thongsawat S, Sirinthornpunya S, Ovartlarnporn B, Kachintorn U, Leelakusolvong S, Kositchaiwat C, Chakkaphak S, Gonlachanvit S. Normal solid gastric emptying values measured by scintigraphy using Asian-style meal: a multicenter study in healthy volunteers. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 20(3):371-378, 2014. doi:10.5056/jnm13114
- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
- Degen LP, Phillips SF. Variability of gastrointestinal transit in healthy women and men. Gut 39(2):299-305, 1996. doi:10.1136/gut.39.2.299
- Hutson WR, Roehrkasse RL, Wald A. Influence of gender and menopause on gastric emptying and motility. Gastroenterology 96(1):11-17, 1989. doi:10.1016/0016-5085(89)90758-0
- Grybäck P, Hermansson G, Lyrenäs E, Beckman KW, Jacobsson H, Hellström PM. Nationwide standardisation and evaluation of scintigraphic gastric emptying: reference values and comparisons between subgroups in a multicentre trial. Eur J Nucl Med 27(6):647-655, 2000. doi:10.1007/s002590050558
- Karrento K, Chumpitazi BP, Zhang L, Simpson P, Shulman RJ. 13C-Spirulina gastric emptying breath test normative values in healthy children: a multicenter study with comparison to symptomatic children. Am J Gastroenterol 121(2):491-500, 2026 (epub 2025). doi:10.14309/ajg.0000000000003545
- Hauser B, Roelants M, De Schepper J, Veereman G, Caveliers V, Devreker T, De Greef E, Vandenplas Y. Gastric emptying of solids in children: reference values for the 13C-octanoic acid breath test. Neurogastroenterol Motil 28(10):1480-1487, 2016. doi:10.1111/nmo.12845
```
  fsex_t_colon notes ```
The factor of the sex on the transit times of the large intestine of the female individual
of 35 years relative to a man of the same body weight and height (`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences`): The colonic
transit of women is slower than of men: ICRP 89 para 277, the colonic transit time
of the adult females one third greater than of the males (48 and 36 h, 16 and 12
h for each of the right colon, the left colon and the rectosigmoid); radio-opaque
markers, Metcalf 1987 (the segmental transits of the library, 35 h of a group of
both sexes; the men significantly faster over the whole colon), Meier 1995 (n = 164,
1.40), Degen 1996 (colonic transit faster in men). The factor on the transit times
of every segment of the large intestine (caecum to rectum); the small intestine does
not differ (ICRP 89 para 276, Degen 1996). Children and adolescents 1 (Velde 2013,
no sex difference at 3-18 years), linear to the adults from 15 to 20 years (a choice),
constant after the menopause (no consistent change).

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
- Metcalf AM, Phillips SF, Zinsmeister AR, MacCarty RL, Beart RW, Wolff BG. Simplified assessment of segmental colonic transit. Gastroenterology 92(1):40-47, 1987. doi:10.1016/0016-5085(87)90837-7
- Meier R, Beglinger C, Dederding JP, Meyer-Wyss B, Fumagalli M, Rowedder A, Turberg Y, Brignoli R. Influence of age, gender, hormonal status and smoking habits on colonic transit time. Neurogastroenterol Motil 7(4):235-238, 1995. doi:10.1111/j.1365-2982.1995.tb00231.x
- Degen LP, Phillips SF. Variability of gastrointestinal transit in healthy women and men. Gut 39(2):299-305, 1996. doi:10.1136/gut.39.2.299
- Van de Velde S, Notebaert A, Meersschaut V, Herregods N, Van Winckel M, Van Biervliet S. Colon transit time in healthy children and adolescents. Int J Colorectal Dis 28(12):1721-1724, 2013. doi:10.1007/s00384-013-1750-5
```
  BMI notes ```
Body mass index of the individual.

`BMI = (BW / 1 kg) / (HEIGHT / 100 cm)^2 * 1 kg_per_m2` 

Sources:

- Janmahasatian S, Duffull SB, Ash S, Ward LC, Byrne NM, Green B. Quantification of lean bodyweight. Clin Pharmacokinet 44(10):1051-1065, 2005. doi:10.2165/00003088-200544100-00004
```
  BSA notes ```
Body surface area of the individual, Du Bois formula.

`BSA = 0.007184 m2 * (BW / 1 kg)^0.425 * (HEIGHT / 1 cm)^0.725` 

Sources:

- Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. Clinical calorimetry, tenth paper: a formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 17(6_2):863-871, 1916. doi:10.1001/archinte.1916.00080130010002
```
  FFM notes ```
Fat-free mass of the individual, the female formula of Janmahasatian 2005 times the
factor of the children (Al-Sallami 2015, 1 from 20 years); Huisinga 2012 (eq. 6)
use it for the lean body weight.

`FFM = BW * 9270 dimensionless / (8780 dimensionless + 244 dimensionless * BMI / 1 kg_per_m2) * fage_FFM` 

Sources:

- Janmahasatian S, Duffull SB, Ash S, Ward LC, Byrne NM, Green B. Quantification of lean bodyweight. Clin Pharmacokinet 44(10):1051-1065, 2005. doi:10.2165/00003088-200544100-00004
- Al-Sallami HS, Goulding A, Grant A, Taylor R, Holford N, Duffull SB. Prediction of fat-free mass in children. Clin Pharmacokinet 54(11):1169-1178, 2015. doi:10.1007/s40262-015-0277-z
- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  SV_tis notes ```
Scaling factor of the lean organs but brain and skin, of the blood volume and of
the cardiac output: the fat-free mass less brain and skin relative to the reference
individual (Huisinga 2012 eq. 12, with the tissue masses without blood).

`SV_tis = (FFM - Mbr - Msk) / (FFM_ref - Mbr_ref - Msk_ref)` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mbr notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it is constant (Huisinga 2012 eq. 9).

`Mbr = Mbr_ref` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Msk notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the body surface area
(Huisinga 2012 eq. 10; Huisinga use the Mosteller formula of the body surface area,
the model the Du Bois formula).

`Msk = Msk_ref * BSA / BSA_ref` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Vblood notes ```
Blood volume of the individual; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga 2012 eq.
12, blood is one of the remaining tissues).

`Vblood = Vblood_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mlu notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mlu = Mlu_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mhe notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mhe = Mhe_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mli notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mli = Mli_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mki notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12). Times `fimp_Mki`, the factor of the impaired organ function (`pkpdsim.pbpk.impairment`).

`Mki = (Mki_ref * SV_tis) * fimp_Mki` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  fimp_Mki notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the kidney mass
(adipose, the remainder, takes the difference).

`fimp_Mki = fckd_kidney_volume`
```
  Mst notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mst = Mst_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Msi notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Msi = Msi_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mla notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mla = Mla_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Msp notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Msp = Msp_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mpa notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mpa = Mpa_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mmu notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mmu = Mmu_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mbo notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mbo = Mbo_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mgo notes ```
Mass of the organ tissue without its blood; it scales with the lean tissues (Huisinga
2012 eq. 12).

`Mgo = Mgo_ref * SV_tis` 

Sources:

- Huisinga W, Solms A, Fronton L, Pilari S. Modeling interindividual variability in physiologically based pharmacokinetics and its link to mechanistic covariate modeling. CPT Pharmacometrics Syst Pharmacol 1(9):e4, 2012. doi:10.1038/psp.2012.3
```
  Mlean notes ```
Mass of the tissues of the lean organs without their blood.

`Mlean = Mlu + Mhe + Mbr + Mli + Mki + Mst + Msi + Mla + Msp + Mpa + Mmu + Msk + Mbo + Mgo` 

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  Mad notes ```
Mass of adipose tissue without its blood, the remainder of the body weight; it includes
the tissues of ICRP 89 which are not organs of the model. Density of blood:

- female: Section 7.2.1, specific gravity of whole blood


`Mad = BW - Mlean - Vblood * 1.06 kg_per_l` 

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  f_int_lu notes ```
Interstitial fraction of the organ volume: the interstitial fraction of the tissue
without blood times the tissue share of the organ volume; it is the `f_int` of physiology_human.toml
for the reference individual.

- female: f_int / (1 - f_vas) = 0.09256 / (1 - 0.5372), the interstitial fraction of the tissue without blood; derived: Fint\*(1 - f_vas), Fint = 0.2 (httk 2.7.4 tissue.data, Human, reference Schmitt 2008, Fint of lung); read in httk (MIT), Schmitt 2008 itself not read


`f_int_lu = 0.2 dimensionless * (1 dimensionless - f_vas_lu)` 

Sources:

- Pearce RG, Setzer RW, Strope CL, Wambaugh JF, Sipes NS. httk: R package for high-throughput toxicokinetics. J Stat Softw 79(4):1-26, 2017. R package httk version 2.7.4 (CRAN, 2025-12-08), MIT license, copyright 2025 John Wambaugh; the data set mecdt of HTTK-Pop (Ring 2017) holds the preprocessed NHANES 2013-2018 respondents. doi:10.18637/jss.v079.i04
```
