  fage_GFR notes ```
1 at 35 years, the age of the individual of a build without the physiology over age
(`PBPKDefinition.age`, which gives the curve of `pkpdsim.pbpk.age`): maturation: Table 2, the theoretical allometric
model using normal fat mass (923 GFR measurements of 923 subjects from 22 weeks of
postmenstrual age to 31 years, inulin, 51Cr-EDTA, 99mTc-DTPA and iohexol): half of
the adult GFR at 47.7 weeks of postmenstrual age (95 % CI 45.1-50.5), Hill coefficient
3.40 (3.03-3.80), adult GFR 121 ml/min (117-125). At term (40 weeks of postmenstrual
age) the function is 0.354, which is the 35 % of the asymptotic adult value the paper
states, and at 1 year of postnatal age the predicted GFR is 90 % of the adult. Sex
was not associated with any systematic difference, postnatal age added nothing over
postmenstrual age, and the fat-free mass described the size better than the total
body weight. The size term of this column is (WT/70)^0.75 while the library scales
the GFR with the body surface area; Table 2 also gives the parameters estimated with
a body surface area size model, TM50 52.7 weeks (48.9-56.6), Hill 3.30 (2.92-3.78)
and an adult GFR of 101 ml/min (97-104), which would be the self-consistent pairing
for the library and would make a term newborn 0.287 instead of 0.354 of the adult.
The library keeps the normal fat mass column, which is the parameterisation of the
paper and of every model derived from it, because Rhodin 2009 judge the body surface
area scaling clearly worse and compute the body surface area by the Boyd formula
from weight alone (their equation on p. 69), not by the Du Bois formula of the library;
the body surface area column is recorded here as the alternative and is not used;
aging: as men to 50 years (Delanaye 2022, Pottel 2017); from 50 years the slope of
the healthy women of Eriksen 2020 (Table 3), -0.00866 per year; linear, extrapolated
beyond 95 years

Sources:

- Rhodin MM, Anderson BJ, Peters AM, Coulthard MG, Wilkins B, Cole M, Chatelut E, Grubb A, Veal GJ, Keir MJ, Holford NHG. Human renal function maturation: a quantitative description using weight and postmenstrual age. Pediatr Nephrol 24(1):67-76, 2009. doi:10.1007/s00467-008-0997-5
- Delanaye P, Gaillard F, van der Weijden J, Mjøen G, Ferhman-Ekholm I, Dubourg L, Ebert N, Schaeffner E, Åkerfeldt T, Goffin K, et al. Age-adapted percentiles of measured glomerular filtration in healthy individuals: extrapolation to living kidney donors over 65 years. Clin Chem Lab Med 60(3):401-407, 2022. doi:10.1515/cclm-2021-1011
```
  GFR_base notes ```
GFR of the individual at renal function 1: the reference GFR, which is roughly proportional
to body size (ICRP 89 Section 8.3.1), scaled with the body surface area, i.e. the
same GFR per 1.73 m2, times the factor of the age `fage_GFR` (the maturation of the children
and the decline of the adults). A model which needs the GFR of the kidney without
chronic kidney disease reads it (defined at renal function 0, where the GFR is 0).
Times `fimp_GFR`, the factor of the impaired organ function (`pkpdsim.pbpk.impairment`).

`GFR_base = (GFR_ref * BSA / 1.6440560447723598 m2 * fage_GFR) * fimp_GFR` 

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  fimp_GFR notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the GFR (heart failure:
the GFR follows the renal blood flow; chronic kidney disease is `f_renal_function`).

`fimp_GFR = fcp_gfr * fhf_kidney_flow`
```
  GFR notes ```
GFR of the individual: the GFR at renal function 1 `GFR_base` times the renal function.

`GFR = GFR_base * f_renal_function` 

Sources:

- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
```
  CP_severity notes ```
0 healthy, 1, 2, 3 the Child-Pugh classes A, B, C; between the classes every factor
interpolates linearly. An initial value, as the covariates.
```
  HF_severity notes ```
0 healthy, 1 to 4 the NYHA classes I to IV; between the classes every factor interpolates
linearly. An initial value, as the covariates.
```
  CKD_hepatic notes ```
Switch (0 off, 1 on) of the reduced activity of the hepatic CYP2D6 and OATP1B of
chronic kidney disease (by the stage of `f_renal_function`).
```
  fcp_hepatic_artery_flow notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the hepatic artery flow (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes
A, B, C, linear in between), the values of the classes: A 1.3, B 2.3, C 3.4; hepatic
arterial blood flow relative to healthy (hepatic arterial buffer response): Edginton
& Willmann 2008, Table I, from Annet 2003 with the control set at 160 mL/min,
the ratios of the mean arterial perfusion per volume of liver of Annet 2003 (1.29,
2.35, 3.42); the consensus of the database on the arterial perfusion (Annet 2003,
Van Beers 2001: 1.13, 2.07, 3.26) times that on the liver volume per body weight
(0.90, 0.79, 0.67) gives 1.01, 1.63, 2.17, and the absolute flow of the hepatic artery
of Sudhamshu 2006 gives 1.40, 1.56, 1.80 (`pkpdsim.database.integration.consensus`); kept until the flow of the hepatic
artery is decided together with the shunt and the functional liver

`fcp_hepatic_artery_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.30000000000000004 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 1.3 dimensionless + 0.9999999999999998 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 2.3 dimensionless + 1.1 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 3.4 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Edginton AN, Willmann S. Physiology-based simulations of a pathological condition: prediction of pharmacokinetics in patients with liver cirrhosis. Clin Pharmacokinet 47(11):743-752, 2008. doi:10.2165/00003088-200847110-00005
```
  fcp_kidney_flow notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the kidney flow (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A,
B, C, linear in between), the values of the classes: A 0.88, B 0.65, C 0.48; renal
blood flow relative to healthy: Edginton & Willmann 2008, Table I, from the inverse
of the renal flow index of Dincer 2005

`fcp_kidney_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.12 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.88 dimensionless + -0.22999999999999998 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.65 dimensionless + -0.17000000000000004 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.48 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Edginton AN, Willmann S. Physiology-based simulations of a pathological condition: prediction of pharmacokinetics in patients with liver cirrhosis. Clin Pharmacokinet 47(11):743-752, 2008. doi:10.2165/00003088-200847110-00005
```
  fcp_cardiac_index notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the cardiac index (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A,
B, C, linear in between), the values of the classes: A 1.099, B 1.21, C 1.36; cardiac
index relative to healthy (hyperdynamic circulation); the flows of the organs without
a factor of their own (heart, muscle, skin, adipose, bone, gonads) close the cardiac
output to it; Edginton & Willmann 2008 (Table I) give 1.11, 1.27 and 1.36 (the
cardiac index of Dincer 2005, observed 1.19, 1.29, 1.43 of the controls), and 1.75,
2.25 and 2.75 for the flow of the other organs; the consensus of the studies of the
parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the cardiac output per body surface area (cardiac_index)
of the class to the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects
meta-analysis; A 1.1 (95 % CI 1.01-1.19; 3 studies: Dincer2005 1.18, Henriksen2001
1.05, Iwao1997 1.09), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
B 1.21 (95 % CI 1.1-1.33; 3 studies: Dincer2005 1.28, Henriksen2001 1.13, Iwao1997
1.26), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; C 1.36 (95 % CI
1.25-1.48; 3 studies: Dincer2005 1.42, Henriksen2001 1.27, Iwao1997 1.44), from a
physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects

`fcp_cardiac_index = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.09899999999999998 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 1.099 dimensionless + 0.11099999999999999 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 1.21 dimensionless + 0.15000000000000013 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 1.36 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Dincer D, Besisk F, Demirkol O, Demir K, Kaymakoglu S, Cakaloglu Y, Okten A. Relationships between hemodynamic alterations and Child-Pugh Score in patients with cirrhosis. Hepatogastroenterology 52(65):1521-1525, 2005. PMID 16201110.
- Henriksen JH, Bendtsen F, Møller S. Normal red cell cardiac output in the hyperkinetic syndrome of alcoholic cirrhosis. J Hepatol 34(5):782-783, 2001. doi:10.1016/s0168-8278(01)00019-8
- Iwao T, Oho K, Sakai T, Tayama C, Sato M, Nakano R, Yamawaki M, Toyonaga A, Tanikawa K. Splanchnic and extrasplanchnic arterial hemodynamics in patients with cirrhosis. J Hepatol 27(5):817-823, 1997. doi:10.1016/s0168-8278(97)80318-2
```
  fcp_gfr notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the gfr (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B, C, linear
in between), the values of the classes: A 1.0, B 0.7, C 0.36; glomerular filtration
rate relative to healthy (hepatorenal reduction): Edginton & Willmann 2008, Table
I, from inulin clearance: class A as the controls, class B a value of the range of
the review of Proulx et al., class C from Woitas 2000; the GFR of classes B and C
varies from 0.18 of the controls to none, Edginton & Willmann 2008

`fcp_gfr = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.0 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.30000000000000004 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.7 dimensionless + -0.33999999999999997 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.36 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Edginton AN, Willmann S. Physiology-based simulations of a pathological condition: prediction of pharmacokinetics in patients with liver cirrhosis. Clin Pharmacokinet 47(11):743-752, 2008. doi:10.2165/00003088-200847110-00005
```
  fcp_albumin notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the albumin (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B, C,
linear in between), the values of the classes: A 0.8645, B 0.6798, C 0.4892; plasma
albumin relative to healthy, the binding protein factor of fu; Edginton & Willmann
2008 (Table I) give 0.81, 0.68 and 0.50 (from Chawla 1998, Froomes 1999 and Woitas
2000, which the consensus has with the further studies); the consensus of the studies
of the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the plasma (serum) albumin concentration
(albumin) of the class to the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by
a random-effects meta-analysis; A 0.865 (95 % CI 0.808-0.925; 4 studies: Chawla1998
0.807, Mesropyan2022 0.879, Viani1992 0.839, Yang2020 0.913), from a physiological
or clinical quantity of the subjects; B 0.68 (95 % CI 0.633-0.73; 5 studies: Chawla1998
0.673, Froomes1999 0.626, Mesropyan2022 0.63, Viani1992 0.652, Yang2020 0.761), from
a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; C 0.489 (95 % CI 0.43-0.556;
5 studies: Chawla1998 0.488, Froomes1999 0.451, Mesropyan2022 0.537, Viani1992 0.432,
Yang2020 0.567), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects

`fcp_albumin = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.13549999999999995 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.8645 dimensionless + -0.1847000000000001 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.6798 dimensionless + -0.19059999999999994 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.4892 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Chawla Y, Radha NS, Jang KD, Dilawari B. Portal Hemodynamics by Duplex Doppler Sonography in Different Grades of Cirrhosis. Dig Dis Sci 43(2):354-357, 1998. doi:10.1023/a:1018814624307
- Mesropyan N, Kupczyk PA, Dold L, Praktiknjo M, Chang J, Isaak A, Endler C, Kravchenko D, Bischoff LM, Sprinkart AM, Pieper CC, Kuetting D, Jansen C, Attenberger UI, Luetkens JA. Assessment of liver cirrhosis severity with extracellular volume fraction MRI. Sci Rep 12(1):9422, 2022. doi:10.1038/s41598-022-13340-9
- Viani A, Rizzo G, Carrai M, Pacifici GM. Interindividual variability in the concentrations of albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein in patients with renal or liver disease, newborns and healthy subjects: implications for binding of drugs. Int J Clin Pharmacol Ther Toxicol 30(4):128-133, 1992. PMID 1572757.
- Yang M, Zhang Y, Zhao W, Cheng W, Wang H, Guo S. Evaluation of liver function using liver parenchyma, spleen and portal vein signal intensities during the hepatobiliary phase in Gd-EOB-D TPA-enhanced MRI. BMC Med Imaging 20(1):119, 2020. doi:10.1186/s12880-020-00519-7
- Froomes PR, Morgan DJ, Smallwood RA, Angus PW. Comparative effects of oxygen supplementation on theophylline and acetaminophen clearance in human cirrhosis. Gastroenterology 116(4):915-920, 1999. doi:10.1016/s0016-5085(99)70075-2
```
  fcp_agp notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the agp (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B, C, linear
in between), the values of the classes: A 0.6, B 0.56, C 0.3; plasma alpha-1-acid
glycoprotein relative to healthy, the binding protein factor of fu: Edginton &
Willmann 2008, Table I, from Barry et al.

`fcp_agp = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.4 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.6 dimensionless + -0.039999999999999925 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.56 dimensionless + -0.26000000000000006 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.3 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Edginton AN, Willmann S. Physiology-based simulations of a pathological condition: prediction of pharmacokinetics in patients with liver cirrhosis. Clin Pharmacokinet 47(11):743-752, 2008. doi:10.2165/00003088-200847110-00005
```
  fcp_hematocrit notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the hematocrit (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B,
C, linear in between), the values of the classes: A 0.9232, B 0.7411, C 0.7232; hematocrit
relative to healthy (the anaemia of cirrhosis); Edginton & Willmann 2008 (Table
I) give absolute hematocrits of 0.39, 0.37 and 0.35 with the controls at 0.43 (0.91,
0.86, 0.81 of the controls, from Wong 2001); the consensus of the studies of the
parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the hematocrit (hematocrit) of the class to
the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis;
A 0.923 (95 % CI 0.892-0.956; 2 studies: Mesropyan2022 0.915, Wong2001 0.949), from
a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; B 0.741 (95 % CI 0.712-0.771;
1 study: Mesropyan2022 0.741), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
C 0.723 (95 % CI 0.612-0.854; 2 studies: Boker2000 0.789, Mesropyan2022 0.666), from
a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects

`fcp_hematocrit = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.07679999999999998 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.9232 dimensionless + -0.18210000000000004 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.7411 dimensionless + -0.017900000000000027 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.7232 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Mesropyan N, Kupczyk PA, Dold L, Praktiknjo M, Chang J, Isaak A, Endler C, Kravchenko D, Bischoff LM, Sprinkart AM, Pieper CC, Kuetting D, Jansen C, Attenberger UI, Luetkens JA. Assessment of liver cirrhosis severity with extracellular volume fraction MRI. Sci Rep 12(1):9422, 2022. doi:10.1038/s41598-022-13340-9
- Wong F, Girgrah N, Graba J, Allidina Y, Liu P, Blendis L. The cardiac response to exercise in cirrhosis. Gut 49(2):268-275, 2001. doi:10.1136/gut.49.2.268
- Böker KH, Franzen A, Wrobel M, Bahr MJ, Tietge U, Manns MP. Regulation of hepatic blood flow in patients with liver cirrhosis and after liver transplantation. Pathophysiology 6(4):251-260, 2000. doi:10.1016/s0928-4680(99)00024-3
```
  fcp_cyp3a4 notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the activity of CYP3A4 in the liver (abundance per amount
of liver tissue) (CP_severity 1, 2, 3 the classes A, B, C, linear in between), the
values of the classes: A 0.5981, B 0.3449, C 0.195; the consensus of the studies
of the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the abundance per amount of liver tissue
of CYP3A4 of the class to the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by
a random-effects meta-analysis; A 0.598 (95 % CI 0.346-1.03; 2 studies: Drozdzik2021
0.598, ElKhateeb2021 0.595); B 0.345 (95 % CI 0.181-0.659; 2 studies: Drozdzik2021
0.38, ElKhateeb2021 0.232); C 0.195 (95 % CI 0.101-0.376; 3 studies: Drozdzik2021
0.312, ElKhateeb2021 0.205, Prasad2018 0.13)

`fcp_cyp3a4 = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, CP_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.40190000000000003 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 0 dimensionless), CP_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.5981 dimensionless + -0.2532 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 1 dimensionless), CP_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.3449 dimensionless + -0.14989999999999998 dimensionless * (CP_severity - 2 dimensionless), CP_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.195 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Drozdzik M, Lapczuk-Romanska J, Wenzel C, Szelag-Pieniek S, Post M, Skalski Ł, Kurzawski M, Oswald S. Gene expression and protein abundance of hepatic drug metabolizing enzymes in liver pathology. Pharmaceutics 13(9):1334, 2021. doi:10.3390/pharmaceutics13091334
- El-Khateeb E, Achour B, Al-Majdoub ZM, Barber J, Rostami-Hodjegan A. Non-uniformity of changes in drug-metabolizing enzymes and transporters in liver cirrhosis: implications for drug dosage adjustment. Mol Pharm 18(9):3563-3577, 2021. doi:10.1021/acs.molpharmaceut.1c00462
- Prasad B, Bhatt DK, Johnson K, Chapa R, Chu X, Salphati L, Xiao G, Lee C, Hop CE, Mathias A, Lai Y, Liao M, Humphreys WG, Kumer SC, Unadkat JD. Abundance of phase 1 and 2 drug-metabolizing enzymes in alcoholic and hepatitis C cirrhotic livers: a quantitative targeted proteomics study. Drug Metab Dispos 46(7):943-952, 2018. doi:10.1124/dmd.118.080523
```
  fcp_rest_flow notes ```
Factor of liver cirrhosis on the blood flow of the organs without a factor of their
own (heart, muscle, skin, adipose, bone, gonads): the cardiac index less the flows
of the other organs (the portal organs and the brain unchanged, the hepatic artery
and the kidney by their factors) over their share of cardiac output, so that the
flows sum to the cardiac output of the cardiac index.

`fcp_rest_flow = 1 dimensionless + (fcp_cardiac_index - 1 dimensionless - (fQli * (fcp_hepatic_artery_flow - 1 dimensionless) + fQki * (fcp_kidney_flow - 1 dimensionless))) / (fQhe + fQmu + fQsk + fQad + fQbo + fQgo)`
```
  eGFR notes ```
The GFR per 1.73 m2 body surface area of the renal function: the GFR per 1.73 m2
of the female reference individual (114.7 ml/min, the reference GFR scaled to 1.73
m2 with the reference body surface area) times `f_renal_function`; it gives the stage of chronic
kidney disease, the lower limits (ml/min/1.73 m2) of normal, mild, moderate and severe
[90.0, 60.0, 30.0, 15.0]; lower limits of the GFR per 1.73 m2 of normal function
and of mild, moderate and severe CKD (Methods: mild 60-89, moderate 30-59, severe
15-29, end stage < 15 ml/min/1.73 m2, the classes of the FDA guidance)

`eGFR = 0.11469803635928352 l_per_min * f_renal_function` 

Sources:

- Yoshida K, Sun B, Zhang L, Zhao P, Abernethy DR, Nolin TD, Rostami-Hodjegan A, Zineh I, Huang SM. Systematic and quantitative assessment of the effect of chronic kidney disease on CYP2D6 and CYP3A4/5. Clin Pharmacol Ther 100(1):75-87, 2016. doi:10.1002/cpt.337
- Valentin J (ed). Basic anatomical and physiological data for use in radiological protection: reference values. ICRP Publication 89. Ann ICRP 32(3-4), 2002. doi:10.1016/S0146-6453(03)00002-2
- Du Bois D, Du Bois EF. Clinical calorimetry, tenth paper: a formula to estimate the approximate surface area if height and weight be known. Arch Intern Med 17(6_2):863-871, 1916. doi:10.1001/archinte.1916.00080130010002
```
  fckd_plasma_protein notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the plasma protein, linear in the eGFR between
the anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 1.0, moderate
1.45, severe 2.2, end stage 2.5 (mild: a model choice, not a value of the source);
Table 3: the correction factor for the effect of CKD on the content of plasma proteins
of the stages 3, 4 and 5 (30-60, 15-30, < 15 ml/min/1.73 m2), from Malik 2020
(not read); mild CKD (stage 2) is not tabulated, 1 (model choice); the library takes
the factor as the factor by which the binding capacity of the plasma proteins falls
(the binding factor of fu is divided by it, so fu of a strongly bound drug rises
by it), a model choice of the direction

`fckd_plasma_protein = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.44999999999999996 dimensionless * (0.075 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.03 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 1.45 dimensionless + 0.7500000000000002 dimensionless * (0.045 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.0225 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 2.2 dimensionless + 0.2999999999999998 dimensionless * (0.0225 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.015 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 2.5 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Alasmari F, Alasmari MS, Muwainea HM, Alomar HA, Alasmari AF, Alsanea S, Alshamsan A, Rasool MF, Alqahtani F. Physiologically-based pharmacokinetic modeling for single and multiple dosing regimens of ceftriaxone in healthy and chronic kidney disease populations: a tool for model-informed precision dosing. Front Pharmacol 14:1200828, 2023. doi:10.3389/fphar.2023.1200828
- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fckd_hematocrit notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the hematocrit, linear in the eGFR between the
anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 0.9349, moderate
0.8857, severe 0.7909, end stage 0.7106; hematocrit relative to healthy (the anaemia
of CKD): the ratio of the hemoglobin, the hematocrit taken proportional to it (a
constant mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, a model choice); Alasmari 2023
(Table 3) tabulate absolute hematocrits of 0.44, 0.40 and 0.34 for the stages 3,
4 and 5 (from Malik 2020, not read) without the healthy hematocrit of the population;
Malik 2020 (Table 1, after the studies cited there) tabulate the hematocrit by the
creatinine clearance without n or spread, men from 45.5 % (healthy) to 45.2, 44.8,
43.3, 42.6, 41.7 and 34.3 % (CrCl 60-70 to <= 20 ml/min/1.73 m2, 0.99 to 0.75
of healthy), women from 40 % to 33.5 %, milder than the consensus in the moderate
and severe stages; the consensus of the studies of the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the
ratio of the blood hemoglobin concentration (hemoglobin) of the class to the controls
of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis; mild 0.935
(95 % CI 0.908-0.963; 1 study: Panjeta2017 0.935), from a physiological or clinical
quantity of the subjects; moderate 0.886 (95 % CI 0.856-0.916; 2 studies: Lin2017
0.9, Panjeta2017 0.87), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
severe 0.791 (95 % CI 0.764-0.819; 2 studies: Lin2017 0.807, Panjeta2017 0.781),
from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; end stage 0.711 (95 %
CI 0.628-0.804; 3 studies: Lin2017 0.641, Panjeta2017 0.788, Zakiyanov2013 0.713),
from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects

`fckd_hematocrit = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.06510000000000005 dimensionless * (0.09 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.015 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 0.9349 dimensionless + -0.04919999999999991 dimensionless * (0.075 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.03 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 0.8857 dimensionless + -0.0948 dimensionless * (0.045 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.0225 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 0.7909 dimensionless + -0.08030000000000004 dimensionless * (0.0225 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.015 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 0.7106 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Panjeta M, Tahirović I, Sofić E, Ćorić J, Dervišević A. Interpretation of Erythropoietin and Haemoglobin Levels in Patients with Various Stages of Chronic Kidney Disease. J Med Biochem 36(2):145-152, 2017. doi:10.1515/jomb-2017-0014
- Lin HY, Lee YL, Lin KD, Chiu YW, Shin SJ, Hwang SJ, Chen HC, Hung CC. Association of Renal Elasticity and Renal Function Progression in Patients with Chronic Kidney Disease Evaluated by Real-Time Ultrasound Elastography. Sci Rep 7:43303, 2017. doi:10.1038/srep43303
- Zakiyanov O, Kriha V, Vachek J, Zima T, Tesar V, Kalousova M. Placental growth factor, pregnancy-associated plasma protein-A, soluble receptor for advanced glycation end products, extracellular newly identified receptor for receptor for advanced glycation end products binding protein and high mobility group box 1 levels in patients with acute kidney injury: a cross sectional study. BMC Nephrol 14:245, 2013. doi:10.1186/1471-2369-14-245
```
  fckd_kidney_volume notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the kidney volume, linear in the eGFR between
the anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 1.0, moderate
1.0, severe 1.0, end stage 1.0; kidney volume relative to healthy: 1, so that the
loss of nephrons is not counted twice; the kidneys shrink in CKD, the regression
of the log kidney volume on the GFR of Malik 2020 (Figure 1, ln(KV) = -6.3e-5*GFR^2
+ 0.0149*GFR + 4.13, the volumes of Vegar-Zubovic 2016, Jovanovic 2013 and Kim 2010)
gives 0.935, 0.75, 0.59 and 0.486 at the stage anchors relative to an eGFR of 90,
but the capacity of every process of the kidney falls with the renal function already
(`tubular_capacity`, the intact nephron hypothesis) and the processes are per litre of kidney, so
a smaller kidney would lower them a second time; the renal blood flow (`kidney_flow`) is the
measured flow, not a perfusion times a volume. The volumes of the database (Luo 2024
CT: CKD 4-5 0.37 of normal, Buchanan 2020 MRI: CKD 3-4 0.81) group the stages

`fckd_kidney_volume = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fckd_kidney_flow notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the kidney flow, linear in the eGFR between the
anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 0.7712, moderate
0.5567, severe 0.4183, end stage 0.3344; renal blood flow relative to healthy: the
ratio of the perfusion of the renal cortex per tissue mass by arterial spin labelling
MRI, the flow taken proportional to it at the kidney volume of the model (kidney_volume,
1; a model choice: the volume of the kidneys falls in CKD, the database has no volume
per stage against controls, Luo 2024 (CT) and Prasad 2015 (MRI) group the stages);
the measured renal plasma flow of the database (Vukmirovic Papuga 2022, ERPF: mild
0.88, moderate 0.55) agrees; Alasmari 2023 (Table 3) tabulate a renal perfusion of
176, 97 and 75.35 ml/min/100 g for the stages 3 to 5 (from Malik 2020, not read)
without the healthy perfusion; the regression of the log cortical perfusion on the
GFR of Malik 2020 (Figure 1, ln(RCP) = -3e-5*GFR^2 + 0.0170*GFR + 4.09; Buchanan
2019, Gillis 2016, Rossi 2012) gives 0.835, 0.558, 0.399 and 0.313 at the anchors
relative to an eGFR of 90, the consensus agrees; the consensus of the studies of
the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the perfusion of the renal cortex per tissue
mass (arterial spin labelling) (renal_cortical_perfusion) of the class to the controls
of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis; mild 0.771
(95 % CI 0.706-0.843; 2 studies: Brown2020 0.81, ZhangX2024 0.768), from a physiological
or clinical quantity of the subjects; moderate 0.557 (95 % CI 0.505-0.613; 3 studies:
Brown2020 0.624, LiLP2017 0.506, ZhangX2024 0.571), from a physiological or clinical
quantity of the subjects; severe 0.418 (95 % CI 0.303-0.578; 2 studies: Brown2020
0.496, ZhangX2024 0.356), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
end stage 0.334 (95 % CI 0.281-0.398; 2 studies: Brown2020 0.322, ZhangX2024 0.339),
from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects

`fckd_kidney_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.2288 dimensionless * (0.09 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.015 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 0.7712 dimensionless + -0.21450000000000002 dimensionless * (0.075 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.03 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 0.5567 dimensionless + -0.13839999999999997 dimensionless * (0.045 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.0225 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 0.4183 dimensionless + -0.08390000000000003 dimensionless * (0.0225 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.015 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 0.3344 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Brown RS, Sun MRM, Stillman IE, Russell TL, Rosas SE, Wei JL. The utility of magnetic resonance imaging for noninvasive evaluation of diabetic nephropathy. Nephrol Dial Transplant 35(6):970-978, 2020. doi:10.1093/ndt/gfz066
- Zhang X, Ye C, Lu F, Yang J, Xu Y, Wang C. Evaluation of renal oxygenation and perfusion in patients with chronic kidney disease: a preliminary prospective study based on functional magnetic resonance. Ren Fail 46(2):2428337, 2024. doi:10.1080/0886022X.2024.2428337
- Li LP, Tan H, Thacker JM, Li W, Zhou Y, Kohn O, Sprague SM, Prasad PV. Evaluation of Renal Blood Flow in Chronic Kidney Disease Using Arterial Spin Labeling Perfusion Magnetic Resonance Imaging. Kidney Int Rep 2(1):36-43, 2017. doi:10.1016/j.ekir.2016.09.003
```
  fckd_gastric_emptying_time notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the gastric emptying time, linear in the eGFR
between the anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 1.0,
moderate 1.0, severe 1.6, end stage 1.6; gastric emptying time relative to healthy:
Table 2, 1.0 in stage 3 and 1.6 (CV 25 %) in stages 4 and 5 (1.6, CV 30 %, on dialysis),
after Rowland Yeo 2011 and the human studies cited there; stage 2 is not tabulated,
1

`fckd_gastric_emptying_time = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.6000000000000001 dimensionless * (0.045 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.0225 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 1.6 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 1.6 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Malik PRV, Yeung CHT, Ismaeil S, Advani U, Djie S, Edginton AN. A physiological approach to pharmacokinetics in chronic kidney disease. J Clin Pharmacol 60(S1):S52-S62, 2020. doi:10.1002/jcph.1713
```
  fckd_si_transit_time notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the si transit time, linear in the eGFR between
the anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values of the classes: mild 1.0, moderate
1.0, severe 1.4, end stage 1.4; small intestinal transit time relative to healthy:
Table 2, 1.0 in stage 3 and 1.4 (CV 25 %) in stages 4 and 5 (1.8, CV 30 %, on dialysis;
the colonic transit 1.8 on dialysis only); stage 2 is not tabulated, 1

`fckd_si_transit_time = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless + 0.3999999999999999 dimensionless * (0.045 l_per_min - eGFR) / 0.0225 l_per_min, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 1.4 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 1.4 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Malik PRV, Yeung CHT, Ismaeil S, Advani U, Djie S, Edginton AN. A physiological approach to pharmacokinetics in chronic kidney disease. J Clin Pharmacol 60(S1):S52-S62, 2020. doi:10.1002/jcph.1713
```
  fckd_cyp3a4 notes ```
Factor of chronic kidney disease on the activity of CYP3A4 in the liver (with `CKD_hepatic`
= 1), linear in the eGFR between the anchors of the stages (normal 1), the values
of the classes: mild 1.0, moderate 1.0, severe 1.0, end stage 1.0; no apparent relationship
between the severity of CKD and the clearance of CYP3A4/5 model drugs (mean ratios
of the unbound clearance 0.84, 1.05, 0.79, 0.99), taken as unchanged

`fckd_cyp3a4 = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.09 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.045 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0225 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless, eGFR >= 0.0075 l_per_min, 1.0 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Yoshida K, Sun B, Zhang L, Zhao P, Abernethy DR, Nolin TD, Rostami-Hodjegan A, Zineh I, Huang SM. Systematic and quantitative assessment of the effect of chronic kidney disease on CYP2D6 and CYP3A4/5. Clin Pharmacol Ther 100(1):75-87, 2016. doi:10.1002/cpt.337
```
  fhf_liver_flow notes ```
Factor of heart failure on the liver flow of the organs large_intestine, liver, pancreas,
small_intestine, spleen, stomach (HF_severity 1 to 4 the NYHA classes I to IV, linear
in between), the values of the classes: I 0.8728, II 0.7456, III 0.5267, IV 0.4662
(I: a model choice, not a value of the source); hepatic blood flow of the liver and
the portal organs relative to healthy: the indocyanine green clearance of Leithe
1984 (Figure 1C, the subgroups of the cardiac index taken as NYHA II to IV), the
only study with the flow of single classes against controls; NYHA I has no study
and is interpolated; heart failure of several classes against controls in the database:
0.61 (Myers 1948, bromsulphalein), the flow of the superior mesenteric artery in
NYHA II-III 0.74 (Muller 1992); the consensus of the studies of the parameter database
(`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the hepatic blood flow per body surface area (bromsulphalein or
ICG) (hepatic_blood_flow_per_bsa) of the class to the controls of each study on the
log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis; I no study; II 0.746 (95 % CI
0.631-0.881; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.746), from a physiological or clinical quantity
of the subjects; III 0.527 (95 % CI 0.456-0.608; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.527), from
a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; IV 0.466 (95 % CI 0.406-0.536;
1 study: Leithe1984 0.466), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
a class without a study is the linear interpolation between its neighbours (healthy
1) or the last class with a study, a model choice

`fhf_liver_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, HF_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.12719999999999998 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 0 dimensionless), HF_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.8728 dimensionless + -0.12719999999999998 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 1 dimensionless), HF_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.7456 dimensionless + -0.2189000000000001 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 2 dimensionless), HF_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.5267 dimensionless + -0.06049999999999994 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 3 dimensionless), HF_severity < 4 dimensionless, 0.4662 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Leithe ME, Margorien RD, Hermiller JB, Unverferth DV, Leier CV. Relationship between central hemodynamics and regional blood flow in normal subjects and in patients with congestive heart failure. Circulation 69(1):57-64, 1984. doi:10.1161/01.CIR.69.1.57
- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fhf_kidney_flow notes ```
Factor of heart failure on the kidney flow of the organs kidney (HF_severity 1 to
4 the NYHA classes I to IV, linear in between), the values of the classes: I 0.8792,
II 0.7584, III 0.5492, IV 0.6298 (I: a model choice, not a value of the source);
renal blood flow relative to healthy: the PAH clearance of Leithe 1984 (Figure 1E,
the subgroups of the cardiac index taken as NYHA II to IV), not monotonic in the
class (the severe subgroup above the moderate one, as measured); NYHA I has no study
and is interpolated; heart failure of several classes against controls in the database:
the renal plasma flow of advanced congestive failure 0.30 (Mokotoff 1948) and of
stable heart failure of NYHA I-III 0.90 (Jung 2021), the flow of the right renal
artery in NYHA II-III 0.70 (Muller 1992); the consensus of the studies of the parameter
database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the renal blood flow per body surface area (PAH clearance
and hematocrit) (renal_blood_flow_per_bsa) of the class to the controls of each study
on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects meta-analysis; I no study; II 0.758
(95 % CI 0.612-0.939; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.758), from a physiological or clinical
quantity of the subjects; III 0.549 (95 % CI 0.455-0.663; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.549),
from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; IV 0.63 (95 % CI 0.531-0.747;
1 study: Leithe1984 0.63), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
a class without a study is the linear interpolation between its neighbours (healthy
1) or the last class with a study, a model choice

`fhf_kidney_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, HF_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.12080000000000002 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 0 dimensionless), HF_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.8792 dimensionless + -0.12080000000000002 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 1 dimensionless), HF_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.7584 dimensionless + -0.20919999999999994 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 2 dimensionless), HF_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.5492 dimensionless + 0.0806 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 3 dimensionless), HF_severity < 4 dimensionless, 0.6298 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Leithe ME, Margorien RD, Hermiller JB, Unverferth DV, Leier CV. Relationship between central hemodynamics and regional blood flow in normal subjects and in patients with congestive heart failure. Circulation 69(1):57-64, 1984. doi:10.1161/01.CIR.69.1.57
- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fhf_peripheral_flow notes ```
Factor of heart failure on the peripheral flow of the organs adipose, muscle, skin
(HF_severity 1 to 4 the NYHA classes I to IV, linear in between), the values of the
classes: I 0.8244, II 0.6488, III 0.5709, IV 0.4654 (I: a model choice, not a value
of the source); blood flow of skin, adipose and muscle relative to healthy: the limb
(forearm) flow of Leithe 1984 by plethysmography (Figure 1G, the subgroups of the
cardiac index taken as NYHA II to IV); NYHA I has no study and is interpolated; Gu
2025 give 0.57, 0.44 and 0.28 for NYHA II to IV, which is not the limb flow of Leithe
1984 (0.66, 0.57, 0.45 of the normal mean of the text); heart failure of several
classes against controls in the database: forearm in NYHA III-IV 0.46 and 0.51 (Mason
1964, Zelis 1968), calf 0.62 (Zelis 1968) and 0.70 in NYHA II-III (Muller 1992);
the consensus of the studies of the parameter database (`pkpdsim.database.integration`): the ratio of the forearm
blood flow per tissue volume (venous occlusion plethysmography) (forearm_blood_flow)
of the class to the controls of each study on the log scale, pooled by a random-effects
meta-analysis; I no study; II 0.649 (95 % CI 0.496-0.848; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.649),
from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects; III 0.571 (95 % CI 0.448-0.727;
1 study: Leithe1984 0.571), from a physiological or clinical quantity of the subjects;
IV 0.465 (95 % CI 0.38-0.57; 1 study: Leithe1984 0.465), from a physiological or
clinical quantity of the subjects; a class without a study is the linear interpolation
between its neighbours (healthy 1) or the last class with a study, a model choice

`fhf_peripheral_flow = piecewise(1.0 dimensionless, HF_severity <= 0 dimensionless, 1.0 dimensionless + -0.17559999999999998 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 0 dimensionless), HF_severity < 1 dimensionless, 0.8244 dimensionless + -0.17559999999999998 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 1 dimensionless), HF_severity < 2 dimensionless, 0.6488 dimensionless + -0.07790000000000008 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 2 dimensionless), HF_severity < 3 dimensionless, 0.5709 dimensionless + -0.10549999999999998 dimensionless * (HF_severity - 3 dimensionless), HF_severity < 4 dimensionless, 0.4654 dimensionless)` 

Sources:

- Leithe ME, Margorien RD, Hermiller JB, Unverferth DV, Leier CV. Relationship between central hemodynamics and regional blood flow in normal subjects and in patients with congestive heart failure. Circulation 69(1):57-64, 1984. doi:10.1161/01.CIR.69.1.57
- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  fsex_albumin notes ```
The plasma albumin of the female build relative to the men of the same age (`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences`):
The plasma albumin of the women is lower than of the men from puberty to about 55
years: NHANES 2013-2018, survey-weighted means, 0.93-0.95 at 18-49 years, 0.98-0.99
from 55 years, 0.997 at 12 years (blood_nhanes.toml), Weaving 2016 (n = 1,079,193,
the values of the women close to the men at 60 years); not an effect of the oral
contraceptives (NHANES women of 20-49 years without estrogen 0.943). The curve of
the women over age is relative to the women of 35 years (age_human.toml); the constant
ratio of the women to the men at 35 years, 41.65/44.32 = 0.9398, times it gives the
ratio of NHANES at every age from 12 years and 0.997 below, so puberty and the menopause
need no switch.

`fsex_albumin = 0.9398199336597693 dimensionless` 

Sources:

- Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Health Statistics. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) 2013-2014, 2015-2016 and 2017-2018, demographics (DEMO), body measures (BMX), complete blood count (CBC) and standard biochemistry profile (BIOPRO) of the cycles H, I and J (public domain). https://wwwn.cdc.gov/nchs/nhanes/ (accessed 2026-11-06). Read from the public files by scripts/database/nhanes_populations.py, scripts/database/nhanes_blood.py and scripts/database/nhanes_waist.py.
- Weaving G, Batstone GF, Jones RG. Age and sex variation in serum albumin concentration: an observational study. Ann Clin Biochem 53(1):106-111, 2016. doi:10.1177/0004563215593561
```
  fsex_li_cyp3a4 notes ```
The activity of CYP3A4 in the liver of the female build relative to the men of the
same age, per amount of tissue (`pkpdsim.pbpk.sex_differences`): Every kind of evidence finds the women higher:
the protein of the liver 2.0-2.3-fold by immunoblot (Wolbold 2003, 94 livers; verapamil
N-dealkylation +50 %) and 1.18 by LC-MS/MS (Gaither 2025, n = 94, n.s.), the mRNA
of GTEx 1.33-1.76, the activity of liver microsomes 1.24-1.55, the P-gp-independent
marker 4beta-hydroxycholesterol +21 % (n = 1440, adjusted); midazolam (meta-analysis
of Hu and Zhao 2010) oral clearance per kg +16 %, systemic +20 %. With the liver
mass and the flows of the female build 1.2 gives +14 % (oral) and +15 % (systemic)
per kg, without a factor -5 % and 0 to +4 %; the intestine alike (Paine 2005), the
P-gp of liver and intestine alike, so not a P-gp effect (Cummins 2002). No change
at the menopause (protein, Wolbold 2003; midazolam, Gorski 2000; erythromycin breath
test, Harris 1996; no effect of hormone replacement) and no difference in the children
(Parvez 2024).

`fsex_li_cyp3a4 = 1.2 dimensionless` 

Sources:

- Hu ZY, Zhao YS. Sex-dependent differences in cytochrome P450 3A activity as assessed by midazolam disposition in humans: a meta-analysis. Drug Metab Dispos 38(5):817-823, 2010. doi:10.1124/dmd.109.031328
- Wolbold R, Klein K, Burk O, Nüssler AK, Neuhaus P, Eichelbaum M, Schwab M, Zanger UM. Sex is a major determinant of CYP3A4 expression in human liver. Hepatology 38(4):978-988, 2003. doi:10.1053/jhep.2003.50393
- Gaither KA, Yue G, Singh DK, Trudeau J, Ponraj K, Davydova NY, Lazarus P, Davydov DR, Prasad B. Effects of chronic alcohol intake on the composition of the ensemble of drug-metabolizing enzymes and transporters in the human liver. J Xenobiot 15(1):20, 2025. doi:10.3390/jox15010020
```
  fimp_albumin notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the plasma albumin.

`fimp_albumin = fcp_albumin * fsex_albumin`
```
  fimp_agp notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the plasma alpha-1-acid
glycoprotein.

`fimp_agp = fcp_agp`
```
  fimp_plasma_protein notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the binding capacity
of the plasma proteins: it divides the binding factor of fu, so fu of a strongly
bound drug rises by it.

`fimp_plasma_protein = fckd_plasma_protein`
```
  fimp_t_stomach notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the gastric emptying
time.

`fimp_t_stomach = fckd_gastric_emptying_time`
```
  fimp_t_si notes ```
The factor of all impairments (cirrhosis, CKD, heart failure) on the transit times
of the small intestine.

`fimp_t_si = fckd_si_transit_time`
```
  fimp_li_cyp3a4 notes ```
The factor of the individual (the impairments, the age and the sex) on the activity
of CYP3A4 in the liver, the input `fimp_cyp3a4` of the organ; its rates run with `f_cyp3a4` times it.

`fimp_li_cyp3a4 = fcp_cyp3a4 * (1 dimensionless + CKD_hepatic * (fckd_cyp3a4 - 1 dimensionless)) * fsex_li_cyp3a4`
```
  fu_ref_api notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the reference individual (binding protein albumin).

Apixaban is 87 % bound to plasma proteins, mainly albumin, the fraction unbound 13.1
% (He2011). The binding does not change in renal impairment, so the CKD factor of
the binding capacity does not act on it (`ckd=False`): &apos;Protein binding was similar (92%-94%)
between healthy controls and ESRD subjects during the on-dialysis and off-dialysis
periods.&apos; (ELIQUIS US prescribing information, revised 04/2025, 12.3 Pharmacokinetics,
Specific Populations, Hemodialysis in ESRD subjects).
```
  fu_api notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the individual: the reference fraction scaled with
the binding protein under impairment (McNamara 2002, `pkpdsim.pbpk.impairment`).

`fu_api = 1 dimensionless / (1 dimensionless + fimp_albumin * (1 dimensionless - fu_ref_api) / fu_ref_api)`
```
  f_reabsorbed_api notes "Fraction of the glomerular filtrate which is reabsorbed into the plasma of the kidney."
  Kp_api notes ```
Whole-tissue (interstitial and intracellular) to plasma coefficient of the organs
without an own value.

The tissue to the total plasma concentration; the volume of distribution at steady
state is about 21 l (Vakkalagadda2016 20.4 l, Frost2021 21.6 l, ELIQUIS US label).

Fitted value.
```
  ftissue_api notes ```
Exchange between plasma and tissue of the whole body of the reference individual;
an organ has the share of its tissue volume. It multiplies `fu*(Cpl - Cti/Kp)`, so its fitted values
differ from those of the hand-written models.

The whole-body exchange of the unbound apixaban, shared over every tissue by volume;
informed by the early phase after an iv dose (Frost2021, Vakkalagadda2016).

Fitted value.
```
  Vti_total_ref_api notes ```
Tissue volume `V<o>*(1 - f_vas_<o>)` of the female reference individual summed over the organs whose
exchange is a share of `ftissue_api`: lung, heart, brain, kidney, stomach, small_intestine,
large_intestine, spleen, pancreas, muscle, skin, adipose, bone, gonads. A constant,
so that the exchange scales with the tissue volume of the individual.
```
  BP_api notes ```
Blood to plasma ratio of a substance which does not enter the erythrocytes; an observer
value, the flows carry plasma only.

`BP_api = 1 dimensionless - HCT`
```
  fu_ref_api_m1 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the reference individual (binding protein unknown).

The renal clearance of O-demethyl apixaban sulfate (M1) relative to the GFR (the
renal clearance of the library is `fu*GFR`); the fraction unbound of O-demethyl apixaban
sulfate (M1) is not known, it is part of the fitted value. Informed by the urinary
M1 and its plasma concentration (Raghavan2009, Frost2015b). Not a binding measurement,
so the CKD factor of the binding capacity of the plasma proteins does not act on
this lumped renal factor (`ckd=False`, as for the glimepiride metabolites): with it the fraction
unbound, and with it the renal and biliary clearance of O-demethyl apixaban sulfate
(M1), rose as the GFR fell.

Fitted value.
```
  fu_api_m1 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the individual: equal to the reference fraction, no
impairment factor applies (the binding protein is unknown and the binding has no
CKD factor).

`fu_api_m1 = fu_ref_api_m1`
```
  f_reabsorbed_api_m1 notes "Fraction of the glomerular filtrate which is reabsorbed into the plasma of the kidney."
  Kp_api_m1 notes ```
Whole-tissue (interstitial and intracellular) to plasma coefficient of the organs
without an own value.

Kp 1 in the liver: the liver holds O-demethyl apixaban sulfate (M1) at the total
concentration of its plasma, as the export of the old liver model on equal concentrations
of liver and plasma; no other tissue (the old model had no tissue distribution of
the metabolites).
```
  BP_api_m1 notes ```
Blood to plasma ratio of a substance which does not enter the erythrocytes; an observer
value, the flows carry plasma only.

`BP_api_m1 = 1 dimensionless - HCT`
```
  fu_ref_api_m2 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the reference individual; the substance does not bind
to plasma proteins.
```
  fu_api_m2 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the individual; the substance does not bind to plasma
proteins.

`fu_api_m2 = fu_ref_api_m2`
```
  BP_api_m2 notes ```
Blood to plasma ratio of a substance which does not enter the erythrocytes; an observer
value, the flows carry plasma only.

`BP_api_m2 = 1 dimensionless - HCT`
```
  fu_ref_api_m7 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the reference individual (binding protein unknown).

The renal clearance of hydroxy apixaban (M7) relative to the GFR (the renal clearance
of the library is `fu*GFR`); the fraction unbound of hydroxy apixaban (M7) is not known,
it is part of the fitted value. Not identifiable (profile likelihood open on both
sides, the estimate 0.00128 near the lower bound 0.001): M7 has urinary and fecal
data only (Raghavan2009), TODO.md. Not a binding measurement, so the CKD factor of
the binding capacity of the plasma proteins does not act on this lumped renal factor
(`ckd=False`, as for the glimepiride metabolites): with it the fraction unbound, and with
it the renal and biliary clearance of hydroxy apixaban (M7), rose as the GFR fell.

Fitted value.
```
  fu_api_m7 notes ```
Fraction unbound in plasma of the individual: equal to the reference fraction, no
impairment factor applies (the binding protein is unknown and the binding has no
CKD factor).

`fu_api_m7 = fu_ref_api_m7`
```
  f_reabsorbed_api_m7 notes "Fraction of the glomerular filtrate which is reabsorbed into the plasma of the kidney."
  Kp_api_m7 notes ```
Whole-tissue (interstitial and intracellular) to plasma coefficient of the organs
without an own value.

Kp 1 in the liver: the liver holds hydroxy apixaban (M7) at the total concentration
of its plasma, as the export of the old liver model on equal concentrations of liver
and plasma; no other tissue (the old model had no tissue distribution of the metabolites).
```
  BP_api_m7 notes ```
Blood to plasma ratio of a substance which does not enter the erythrocytes; an observer
value, the flows carry plasma only.

`BP_api_m7 = 1 dimensionless - HCT`
```
  k_bile notes ```
transit of the bile into the intestinal lumen as one first order process (half-life
69 min): the bile reaches the duodenum directly or after storage in the gallbladder,
which empties with the meals; without a model of the gallbladder the transit is of
the order of the interval of its emptying, a model with enterohepatic circulation
fits it

Source:

- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  ka_api notes ```
Absorption rate constant of the small intestine; a segment absorbs with the factor
of its area, `ka*f_area`; a model fits it.

The absorption of apixaban from every segment, the lower ileum and the colon with
the factors of the regional absorption (Byon2018): a fast onset, an oral solution
peaks at 0.5 hr (Song2015).

Fitted value.
```
  ka_api_m1 notes ```
No absorption: the substance has no oral route, it is not reabsorbed after biliary
excretion unless a model sets it.
```
  ka_api_m2 notes ```
No absorption: the substance has no oral route, it is not reabsorbed after biliary
excretion unless a model sets it.
```
  ka_api_m7 notes ```
No absorption: the substance has no oral route, it is not reabsorbed after biliary
excretion unless a model sets it.
```
  fabs_api notes ```
Fraction of the absorbed flux which enters the body, the rest goes into the feces.

The fraction of the absorbed flux which enters the body, the rest goes into the feces.
Apixaban is absorbed incompletely with a fast onset: &apos;Following administration
of [14C] Apixaban solution, ... ~ 56 % in feces (34% unchanged). Factoring in the
absolute bioavailability of apixaban (F~ 0.5) ...&apos; (ELIQUIS NDA 202155, Clinical
Pharmacology Review, Reference ID 3232801); the segmented intestine gives an incomplete
absorption otherwise only with a slow ka or the recycling of the efflux through the
colon: a refit with the full colon absorption had an iv half-life of 14.8 hr, with
a colon factor alone an F of the solution of 0.97 (of the tablet 0.74) and a CL of
70.6 ml/min.

Fitted value.
```
  f_ka_colon_api notes ```
Factor of the absorption of the segments of the large intestine, `ka*f_area*f_ka_colon`: the regional
absorption profile of the substance, the value of its source Byon2018, not fitted.

Released by a capsule (Enterion) as a solution of 2.5 mg, apixaban gave an AUC(0-t)
about 60 % lower in the distal small bowel and 84 % lower in the ascending colon
than the oral solution, the relative AUC 0.40 and 0.16 (Byon2018, abstract; ELIQUIS
NDA 202155, Clinical Pharmacology Review, study CV185007, Reference ID 3088157);
the label: &apos;absorbed throughout the gastrointestinal tract with the distal small
bowel and ascending colon contributing about 55% of apixaban absorption&apos; (ELIQUIS
US prescribing information, 12.3 Absorption). The distal small bowel is the lower
ileum (`ile2`) of the library, the colon one factor of its segments (caecum to rectum,
the group `colon`). The factors are derived with the model, not fitted: a solution of
2.5 mg put into the lumen of the lower ileum and of the colon ascendens gives the
AUC of `Carm_api` relative to an oral solution of 0.40 and 0.16 with the factors 0.149 (lower
ileum) and 0.0567 (colon) (`regional_absorption.py` of the model, which derives them again after a refit;
`test_the_regional_absorption_is_that_of_byon2018` of `tests/models/test_apixaban.py`), without them 0.99 and 0.93. The factors depend on the fitted absorption
(`ka_api`, `fabs_api`, the efflux `GU__APISEC_k`), so they and the fit were iterated to a common point: the
factors derived on the adopted fit give the same factors. The colon factor replaces
the fitted `f_ka_colon_api`, which the data of the plasma did not determine (profile likelihood
open towards 0).

Source:

- Byon W, Nepal S, Schuster AE, Shenker A, Frost CE. Regional gastrointestinal absorption of apixaban in healthy subjects. J Clin Pharmacol 58(7):965-971, 2018. Abstract read (Europe PMC, 2026-10-29); the full text is not open access. doi:10.1002/jcph.1097
```
  f_ka_ile2_api notes ```
Factor of the absorption of the segment relative to the small intestine, `ka*f_area_ile2*f_ka_ile2`: the
regional absorption profile of the substance.

Released by a capsule (Enterion) as a solution of 2.5 mg, apixaban gave an AUC(0-t)
about 60 % lower in the distal small bowel and 84 % lower in the ascending colon
than the oral solution, the relative AUC 0.40 and 0.16 (Byon2018, abstract; ELIQUIS
NDA 202155, Clinical Pharmacology Review, study CV185007, Reference ID 3088157);
the label: &apos;absorbed throughout the gastrointestinal tract with the distal small
bowel and ascending colon contributing about 55% of apixaban absorption&apos; (ELIQUIS
US prescribing information, 12.3 Absorption). The distal small bowel is the lower
ileum (`ile2`) of the library, the colon one factor of its segments (caecum to rectum,
the group `colon`). The factors are derived with the model, not fitted: a solution of
2.5 mg put into the lumen of the lower ileum and of the colon ascendens gives the
AUC of `Carm_api` relative to an oral solution of 0.40 and 0.16 with the factors 0.149 (lower
ileum) and 0.0567 (colon) (`regional_absorption.py` of the model, which derives them again after a refit;
`test_the_regional_absorption_is_that_of_byon2018` of `tests/models/test_apixaban.py`), without them 0.99 and 0.93. The factors depend on the fitted absorption
(`ka_api`, `fabs_api`, the efflux `GU__APISEC_k`), so they and the fit were iterated to a common point: the
factors derived on the adopted fit give the same factors. The colon factor replaces
the fitted `f_ka_colon_api`, which the data of the plasma did not determine (profile likelihood
open towards 0).

Source:

- Byon W, Nepal S, Schuster AE, Shenker A, Frost CE. Regional gastrointestinal absorption of apixaban in healthy subjects. J Clin Pharmacol 58(7):965-971, 2018. Abstract read (Europe PMC, 2026-10-29); the full text is not open access. doi:10.1002/jcph.1097
```
  FED notes ```
The prandial state: 1 slows the gastric emptying to the stomach transit time of the
fed state (an initial value, an experiment sets it before the simulation).
```
  IVDOSE_api notes ```
Intravenous dose of apixaban, applied into the venous plasma. An experiment sets
the dose or adds to the rest of an earlier one (`add_doses`).
```
  Ri_api notes ```
Rate of a constant intravenous infusion, which adds to the intravenous dose state;
an experiment sets it.
```
  ti_api notes ```
Half-life of the application of the intravenous dose.

half-life of the application of an intravenous bolus, the injection time of the hand-written
models; fast against the distribution, the bolus is in the venous blood within a
minute

Source:

- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  Kiv_api notes ```
Rate of the application of the intravenous dose; a rule, so that a dose protocol
may change `ti_api` between doses.

`Kiv_api = ln(2) / ti_api * 60 s_per_min`
```
  PODOSE_api notes ```
Oral dose of apixaban as tablet: the undissolved drug in the stomach, which dissolves
and empties into the intestine. An experiment sets the dose or adds to the rest of
an earlier one (`add_doses`).
```
  Ka_dis_api notes ```
Dissolution rate of a small undissolved amount; it saturates with the undissolved
amount.

The dissolution of a small dose of the tablet.

Fitted value.
```
  D50_dis_api notes ```
The dissolution rate is `Ka_dis*D50/(D50 + D)` with the undissolved amount `D`.

The undissolved dose of half the dissolution rate: the dissolution saturates with
the dose (solubility), the exposure is less than dose proportional above 10 mg (Frost2013).

Fitted value.
```
  SOLDOSE_api notes ```
Oral dose of apixaban as solution, which enters the stomach lumen dissolved. An experiment
sets the dose or adds to the rest of an earlier one (`add_doses`).
```
  Ka_sol_api notes ```
Swallowing of the solution into the stomach lumen.

application of an oral solution: swallowing and transfer into the intestinal lumen,
fast against the absorption (half-life 0.7 min)

Source:

- Choice of a library of pkpdsim (PBPK or machine perfusion), not a value of the literature; the notes of the value give the reasoning.
```
  w_arm_sk notes ```
Share of the venous outflow of the skin in the arm vein sample (preset &apos;pksim_default&apos;).

PK-Sim&apos;s default peripheral venous blood, documented as a weighted mean of skin
and muscle venous blood with about 70 % contribution from skin (a similar approach
in the literature: Levitt 2004)

Source:

- Open Systems Pharmacology. PK-Sim documentation: Simulations, section on the venous blood outputs (Peripheral Venous Blood). https://docs.open-systems-pharmacology.org/working-with-pk-sim/pk-sim-documentation/pk-sim-simulations (accessed 2026-10-25).
```
  w_arm_mu notes ```
Share of the venous outflow of the muscle in the arm vein sample (preset &apos;pksim_default&apos;).

PK-Sim&apos;s default peripheral venous blood, documented with about 30 % contribution
from muscle

Source:

- Open Systems Pharmacology. PK-Sim documentation: Simulations, section on the venous blood outputs (Peripheral Venous Blood). https://docs.open-systems-pharmacology.org/working-with-pk-sim/pk-sim-documentation/pk-sim-simulations (accessed 2026-10-25).
```
  Car_free_api notes ```
Unbound plasma concentration of the arterial pool. An observer: an assignment rule
which changes no state.

`Car_free_api = fu_api * Car_api`
```
  Car_blood_api notes ```
Blood concentration of the arterial pool. An observer: an assignment rule which changes
no state.

`Car_blood_api = BP_api * Car_api`
```
  Cve_free_api notes ```
Unbound plasma concentration of the venous pool. An observer: an assignment rule
which changes no state.

`Cve_free_api = fu_api * Cve_api`
```
  Cve_blood_api notes ```
Blood concentration of the venous pool. An observer: an assignment rule which changes
no state.

`Cve_blood_api = BP_api * Cve_api`
```
  Cpo_free_api notes ```
Unbound plasma concentration of the portal pool. An observer: an assignment rule
which changes no state.

`Cpo_free_api = fu_api * Cpo_api`
```
  Cpo_blood_api notes ```
Blood concentration of the portal pool. An observer: an assignment rule which changes
no state.

`Cpo_blood_api = BP_api * Cpo_api`
```
  Chv_api notes ```
Plasma concentration of the hepatic vein: the outflow of the liver mixed with the
portosystemic shunt by their flows. An observer: an assignment rule which changes
no state.

`Chv_api = ((1 dimensionless - f_shunt) * Qh * Cout_li_api + f_shunt * (Qha * Car_api + Qpo * Cpo_api)) / Qh`
```
  Cout_li_api notes ```
Plasma concentration of the blood leaving the organ.

`Cout_api = Cpl_api`
